  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1253 PM CDT THU OCT 1 2026\n\nDeep moisture will continue streaming into the region from the\nsouth, although generally dry conditions will persist across much\nof the area through Saturday. Moisture return will gradually\nincrease through the weekend, albeit somewhat delayed as a cold\nfront stalls to the north and Gulf moisture is partially\nintercepted by coastal convection. By Sunday, a shortwave impulse\nis projected to track north-northeastward out of the Gulf into\nsouthwestern Central Alabama. This feature will aid in lifting\nheavier rainfall farther inland rather than confining it to the\ncoast. Monday night into Tuesday, a deepening trough over eastern\nCanada will kick the cold front southward through the area. In\nits wake, a cooler and drier air mass will filter into the\nregion, bringing a return to seasonable temperatures through\nmidweek.\n\nConfidence remains moderate that the approaching cold front will\nstall far enough north to allow Sunday's shortwave to traverse a\nsignificant portion of the local area. This setup is expected to\nproduce widespread rainfall totals of 1 to 2 inches through\nMonday. Given the moderate-to-heavy nature of the precipitation,\nlocally higher amounts exceeding two inches are possible, with\nprobabilities of exceedance around 50 percent across central and\nsouthern portions of the forecast area. PoPs were lowered\nslightly for Saturday to reflect recent guidance trends showing\nreduced coverage, and further reductions may be necessary in\nsubsequent updates.\n\n16",
